 "exercise": {
  "id": "pqp03-ex06",
  "chapter": 3,
  "number": "3.6",
  "title": "A circuit is a diagram",
  "level": 2,
  "statement": "The Circuit Editor builds circuits from gates; its PQP diagram is shown below it with the ∘⊗ layers switched on. (The CNOT is drawn as a green and a red dot joined by a line — a picture PQP explains in Chapter 9. For now, treat the pair as one box with two inputs and two outputs.)\n\n- (a) Write the whole circuit, starting state included, as one formula using ⊗ and composition, in the Dirac Formula Editor, and press **Verify**. Remember that composition is written right to left: $BA$ means *A first*.\n- (b) Add a gate in a new column of the circuit and extend your formula to match.\n- (c) PQP Theorem 3.22 says a diagram is a circuit exactly when it has no directed cycles. Describe a diagram that is not a circuit.",
  "done": "Verify shows ✓ Equivalent for your formula, before and after (b).",
